CLAIM EXTRACTION / INFLATION MAP
================================
Target: Mann, M.E., Bradley, R.S. & Hughes, M.K. (1998). Global-scale temperature patterns and climate forcing over the past six centuries. Nature 392, 779–787. (MBH98)
URL: https://www.meteo.psu.edu/holocene/public_html/shared/articles/mbh98.pdf
Document type: academic paper
Date of map: 2026-07-30

-----------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT WAS LITERALLY MEASURED
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Annual-resolution multiproxy climate indicators (dendroclimatic/tree-ring, ice-core, ice-melt, coral, long historical documentary, and long instrumental series) from a spatially distributed network, calibrated via principal component analysis (PCA) / empirical orthogonal functions (EOFs) against the dominant patterns of the 1902–1980 instrumental surface temperature field (land air + sea surface, ~1,082 grid-points). 

The network size declines with time: 112 indicators back to 1820, 93 to 1760, 74 to 1700, 57 to 1600, 24 to 1450, and 22 to AD 1400. Reconstruction yields spatially resolved annual surface temperature patterns and, by area-weighted averaging, Northern Hemisphere (NH) and global mean annual temperature series. Calibration period 1902–1980; verification against independent early instrumental data (1854–1901 sparse grids and long grid-point series). Uncertainty (standard error) of the NH reconstruction expands backward in time as fewer proxies are available and fewer eigenvectors are resolved. Unit of analysis: reconstructed annual NH mean temperature anomalies relative to the 1902–1980 mean.

Verification state: FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED (text extracted via browse of the PDF; methods, sample sizes, and verification statistics quoted from source).

-----------------------------------------------------------------
2. STRONGEST CLAIM THE SOURCE ITSELF MAKES
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Abstract (strongest headline form):
“Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperatures for three of the past eight years are warmer than any other year since (at least) AD 1400.”

Body (Results; quantified form):
“Taking into account the uncertainties in our NH reconstruction (see Methods), it appears that the years 1990, 1995 and now 1997 (this value recently calculated and not shown) each show anomalies that are greater than any other year back to 1400 at 3 standard errors, or roughly a 99.7% level of certainty.”

Additional body statements preserve hedging:
“almost all years before the twentieth century well below the twentieth-century climatological mean.”
“We note that hemispheric mean values are not associated with globally or hemispherically uniform trends.”

The abstract omits the 3-SE / ~99.7% quantification and the specific years; the body supplies both. Greenhouse-gas dominance is stated as an inference from time-dependent partial correlations: “It is reasonable to infer that greenhouse-gas forcing is now the dominant external forcing of the climate system.”

Verification state: FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
3. WEAKER DEFENSIBLE CLAIM THE EVIDENCE ACTUALLY SUPPORTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Author reconstruction from Field 1:
The multiproxy climate-field reconstruction of Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperature, calibrated on 1902–1980 instrumental data and verified against independent early instrumental series, produces an NH mean series in which the reconstructed values for 1990, 1995 and 1997 exceed every reconstructed annual value back to AD 1400 by more than three standard errors of the reconstruction uncertainty. That uncertainty widens substantially before ~1600 because only 22–57 proxies remain and fewer eigenvectors are retained. The reconstruction captures ~40–50% of NH variance back to 1400 and higher fractions in later centuries; skill is highest for the largest spatial scales. The data therefore support a claim that these three recent years are warmer than any other year in the reconstructed NH series since 1400 at the stated statistical threshold under the authors’ own uncertainty model. The claim does not extend, on this paper’s own design, to the full millennium, to individual grid-points, or to a claim of global uniformity.

Verification state: author reconstruction from Field 1 (FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED source text).

-----------------------------------------------------------------
4. BROADER CLAIM ATTACHED DOWNSTREAM
-----------------------------------------------------------------
RETRIEVED (IPCC Third Assessment Report, Summary for Policymakers, 2001):
“New analyses of proxy data for the Northern Hemisphere indicate that the increase in temperature in the 20th century is likely to have been the largest of any century during the past 1,000 years. It is also likely that, in the Northern Hemisphere, the 1990s was the warmest decade and 1998 the warmest year.”
(Figure 1b of the SPM is based on the reconstructions; the underlying chapter draws on Mann et al. 1998 and especially the 1999 extension.)

RETRIEVED (Wikipedia summary of reception, cross-checked against multiple secondary sources):
The TAR featured a version of the MBH99 graph (the extension of the MBH98 methodology back to AD 1000) and stated that the 1990s was likely the warmest decade and 1998 the warmest year of the past 1,000 years in the Northern Hemisphere.

RETRIEVED (common secondary literature and press phrasing):
Frequent restatements of the form “the warmest year in at least 1,000 years” or “unprecedented in the last millennium,” often citing “Mann et al. 1998” or “Mann et al. 1998, 1999” jointly for the millennial claim.

Attribution check (CROSS-SOURCE-CONFIRMED for the IPCC SPM wording):
The millennial (past 1,000 years) claim cannot be supported by MBH98 alone (which ends at AD 1400). It requires the successor paper Mann, Bradley & Hughes 1999 (MBH99). The TAR text and figure primarily rest on MBH99 while still citing the 1998 paper. Joint citation of the two papers for the 1,000-year claim is therefore SYNTHESIS ATTRIBUTION in documents that distinguish the scopes; in many popular and secondary restatements that cite only “Mann et al. 1998” for a 1,000-year claim, the classification is MISATTRIBUTED.

NONE FOUND for exhaustive enumeration of every press release; representative high-visibility downstream uses (IPCC TAR SPM and subsequent literature) were retrieved.

Field 4 verification: RETRIEVED items are quoted or closely paraphrased from the cited sources; RECALLED material is not used to anchor later fields.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
5. WHERE THE INFLATION BEGINS
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Within-source component (established from paper alone):
The abstract states the “warmer than any other year since (at least) AD 1400” claim without the body ’s explicit 3-standard-error / ~99.7% quantification and without naming the three years. This is a modest prominence asymmetry (abstract vs. results) but does not invent a longer time span or remove the reconstruction’s own uncertainty model.

Downstream component:
- Scope extension from “since AD 1400” (MBH98) to “past 1,000 years / the millennium” (IPCC TAR SPM and most secondary literature). This extension is carried by MBH99; when the millennial claim is attributed solely or primarily to the 1998 paper, it is misattribution.
- Conversion of the specific quantified statement (“three of the past eight years \ldots at 3 standard errors”) into the broader, less quantified “1990s warmest decade / 1998 warmest year of the millennium.”
- Frequent loss of the paper’s own caveats that hemispheric means are not globally uniform and that uncertainty expands backward.

Failure location:
MIXED — PAPER (abstract–body prominence asymmetry on the quantified threshold) + CITATION / ASSESSMENT (IPCC TAR and subsequent literature that extend the period to 1,000 years and attribute the extended claim to the 1998 paper or the joint 1998/1999 pair without always disambiguating).

Anchoring items and provenance:
- Paper abstract and Results paragraphs: FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED (source PDF).
- IPCC TAR SPM wording: RETRIEVED.
- Attribution relationship (MBH98 vs. MBH99 scopes): CROSS-SOURCE-CONFIRMED.

The primary locus of the period-length inflation is therefore the citation / assessment layer, not the 1998 paper’s own measurement claim.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
6. WHICH CLAIM IS THIS AUDIT EVALUATING?
-----------------------------------------------------------------
The quantified claim stated in the Results section of MBH98: that the years 1990, 1995 and 1997 each exceed every other year in the reconstructed Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperature series back to AD 1400 at three standard errors (~99.7% level of certainty under the authors’ uncertainty model).

Rationale for selection (Rule 2): This is the most precisely quantified version of the central temperature-extremes claim. The abstract’s unquantified form and the downstream millennial restatements are secondary; the prosecutors should first test whether the measurement design and uncertainty model actually support the number the authors themselves attached.

